            SortOrder = 1,
            AppRoutes = """["/costing/periods","/admin/costing"]""",
            Tags = """["costing","overhead","finance","standard-cost"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Standard Costing and Overhead\n\nMaterial and direct labor are easy to see on a job. Rent, power, payroll taxes, benefits and the depreciation on your presses are not — yet every part you ship has to carry its share of them. Costing is where you tell Forge what those shared costs are, so the standard-cost rollup on each part includes them instead of pretending they are free.\n\nForge offers two levels. **Departmental rates** is the simple one: a single overhead percentage on top of labor for each work center. **Activity-based costing** (the Costing page under Financials) builds that number up from real budgets so you can see where it comes from. Both are optional and off until an admin turns the capability on.\n\n### The simple way: departmental rates\n\nUnder **Admin > Costing**, flip on **Use departmental overhead rates**. You get one box per active work center: the overhead as a percentage of direct labor. Type `150` and every labor dollar routed through that work center carries $1.50 of overhead in the rollup. Leave a work center blank and it falls back to the flat burden rate per hour already set on the work center itself.\n\nThat is the whole feature. It is a good fit when you already know your overhead percentage from your accountant and just want the rollup to use it.\n\n### The built-up way: Costing under Financials\n\nThe **Costing** page has four tabs. Read them left to right and you have the whole model.\n\n- **Periods** — the window a set of rates applies to, usually a fiscal year. Rates are frozen per period so month-to-month comparisons mean something.\n- **Cost Centers** — the places cost lives: the plant floor, a support department, the warehouse, the office. Each has a type, square footage, headcount and an *inventoriable* flag that says whether its cost belongs in product cost (the floor) or in period cost (the office).\n- **Overhead Pools** — a bucket of one kind of cost inside a cost center: Utilities, Facilities and Rent, Payroll Taxes, Benefits, Equipment. Each pool has a **behavior** (fixed, variable, or semi-variable with a fixed portion) and a **driver** — what spreads it across parts: labor hours, machine hours, labor dollars, material dollars, units or receipt count.\n- **Templates** — the list of questions the quick start asks. The system template ships with the product; you can build your own.\n\n### The quick start\n\nYou do not have to build cost centers and pools by hand. The **Quick start** button in the toolbar asks for the fiscal year, your direct headcount and average hourly wage, then one amount per template line: utilities per month, facilities and rent per month, employer payroll tax as a percent of wages, benefits per employee per month, equipment and depreciation per year.\n\nApply it and Forge creates a **PLANT** cost center, a costing period for that fiscal year, one pool per line, and a budget for each pool sized to your headcount (it assumes 2,080 hours per person per year). Two toggles at the bottom go further: one also creates matching expense accounts and GL budget lines when the full general ledger is on; the other sets a default labor rate (wage plus the per-hour burden) for any user who does not have one yet.\n\nRunning it twice for the same year reuses the PLANT cost center and the period and updates the budgets rather than duplicating them.\n\n### Budgets and the freeze\n\nA pool by itself has no rate. On the Overhead Pools tab, **Set Pool Budget** ties a pool to a period with a **Budget Amount** (dollars for the period) and a **Driver Qty** (how many labor hours, machine hours or units you expect). The rate is simply amount divided by quantity — $52,000 of utilities over 20,800 labor hours is $2.50 per labor hour.\n\nNothing reaches the part rollup until you **Freeze** the period on the Periods tab. Freezing turns every budget into a rate and composes a rate card per work center: labor, labor overhead, machine, variable machine overhead and fixed machine overhead. **View Rates** shows that card, as a table or as a chart. A frozen period stays frozen; open a new period for the next year.\n\n### Templates\n\nThe quick start only knows the questions on its template. Open the **Templates** tab and click **New template** to write your own: give each line a code, a name, a behavior, a driver, how the question is asked (monthly amount, annual amount, per employee per month, or percent of wages), an optional default, and optionally the GL account it should mirror into. The system template cannot be deleted; your own can, and deleting one keeps any budgets it already applied.\n\n### What to do first\n\n1. Ask an admin to turn on **Tier 3 — Activity-based costing** (and **Tier 2 — Departmental cost rates** if you want the simple percentage option too).\n2. Open **Costing**, click **Quick start**, answer the questions from last year's books, and apply.\n3. Check the Overhead Pools tab; adjust any budget that looks wrong.\n4. Freeze the period and open **View Rates** to see the overhead per hour each work center now carries.\n5. Re-roll your standard costs and compare a few parts against what you have been quoting.","sections":[]}"""
        });

